ome infected in each  of these treatments  and i found an interesting trend tighter  and the recipient plant  increases with increasing inhibitor  concentration so again on the x-axis we  have my different inhibitor  concentrations and now on the y-axis  we're looking at titer  how much virus is present in these  plants so for  the zero and 500 micromolar treatments  we have lower tighter than 100  micromolar  of the inhibitor and at the 200  micromolar dose we see that this  difference in titer is now significant  so what does this mean uh for the role  of c1kbp in transmission  so as you recall this is an inhibitor so  inhibits the function of c1 qbp  and it seems when we do increasing  amounts of inhibitor we are also getting  increasing amounts of peeler v titer in  these recipient plants  so this to me suggests that maybe c1 qbp  is somehow a negative regulator a p orb  transmission so its normal function is  to either lower the amount of virus  these aphids are inoculating into these  plants or  that's accumulating in the insect body  possibly um  but why why would c1p do that what could  be  it's a negative regulator but what does  that mean for the function um so i  hypothesized that maybe the role of c1  qpp and immunity may be conserved  um so complement receptors in humans  such as c1 qbp have been previously  shown to recognize pathogen associated  molecular patterns or pamps and the c1  qbp protein itself has been found to  bind several viruses in humans such as  rubella  hiv and herpes simplex virus so  we've shown that you know cmqvpp from  mises binds plrv  c1 qbp from that other api binds three  different polaroviruses  so it seems that maybe uh this protein  is recognizing viruses more generally  so is it possible that c13vpp could be  acting as some sort of pattern  recognition receptor  in aphids um but again why would they  if they're doing that and they're  playing this immunity role why would  aphids  launch an immune response to a virus  that